# Nubia Red Magic 11S Pro+ (NX809J) U-Boot environment.
#
# The panel is the ONLY console on this board: no UART pads, no USB gadget. So
# stdout/stderr go to the video console, which draws into the splash buffer ABL
# left the DPU scanning (0xfc800000), and stdin keeps the always-registered
# "serial" stdio stub so console setup has an input device. It never produces
# input, which is why bootcmd is a self-contained script rather than something
# you interact with.
#
# THREE RULES, each of which cost a flash cycle to learn:
#
#  1. bootcmd is ONE line once the continuations are joined, and
#     cli_simple_run_command() refuses any command not strictly shorter than
#     CONFIG_SYS_CBSIZE: it prints "## Command too long!" and runs NOTHING -
#     no partition lookup, no sysboot, no diagnostics. At 2092 bytes this file
#     silently did exactly that. Keep bootcmd well under the limit (check it
#     with the build repo's envlen.py; the U-Boot workflow asserts it).
#
#  2. There is no hush shell (CONFIG_HUSH_PARSER, CMD_TEST, CMD_LS and CMD_FAT
#     are all off): no if, no test, no ls. bootcmd is a linear list and
#     fallbacks work by letting a failing command print its error and fall
#     through. sysboot does not return when it boots something, so everything
#     after it is the failure path.
#
#  3. `scsi N` is not UFS LUN N. drivers/scsi/scsi.c assigns each block
#     device's devnum automatically, one per detected LUN in LUN order, so a
#     LUN that fails detection shifts the numbering - boot_b (LUN 4 by Android's
#     sdX naming) came up as scsi 5. Look the partition up per device; never
#     hardcode it.
#
# bootdelay is deliberately not set: the defconfig compiles in
# CONFIG_BOOTDELAY=-2, which runs bootcmd immediately with no countdown and no
# way to abort. Load addresses are not set either: board_late_init() allocates
# them out of ABL's real memory map via LMB, after initr_env, so anything here
# would be overwritten.
stdin=serial
stdout=vidconsole
stderr=vidconsole

# One line: does the ramoops zone even exist yet (i.e. did the previous boot
# reach pstore init, or die before it)?
bootcmd=echo; \
md.b 0xe2000000 0xc; \
scsi scan; \
part number scsi 5 boot_b p5; echo "  scsi5 boot_b=[${p5}]"; \
part number scsi 4 boot_b p4; echo "  scsi4 boot_b=[${p4}]"; \
part number scsi 6 boot_b p6; echo "  scsi6 boot_b=[${p6}]"; \
part number scsi 5 boot_a q5; echo "  scsi5 boot_a=[${q5}]"; \
part number scsi 4 boot_a q4; echo "  scsi4 boot_a=[${q4}]"; \
part number scsi 6 boot_a q6; echo "  scsi6 boot_a=[${q6}]"; \
sysboot scsi 5:${p5} fat ${scriptaddr} /extlinux/extlinux.conf; \
sysboot scsi 4:${p4} fat ${scriptaddr} /extlinux/extlinux.conf; \
sysboot scsi 6:${p6} fat ${scriptaddr} /extlinux/extlinux.conf; \
sysboot scsi 5:${q5} fat ${scriptaddr} /extlinux/extlinux.conf; \
sysboot scsi 4:${q4} fat ${scriptaddr} /extlinux/extlinux.conf; \
sysboot scsi 6:${q6} fat ${scriptaddr} /extlinux/extlinux.conf; \
sysboot scsi 3: fat ${scriptaddr} /extlinux/extlinux.conf; \
sysboot scsi 7: fat ${scriptaddr} /extlinux/extlinux.conf; \
sysboot scsi 0: fat ${scriptaddr} /extlinux/extlinux.conf; \
sysboot scsi 1: fat ${scriptaddr} /extlinux/extlinux.conf; \
sysboot scsi 2: fat ${scriptaddr} /extlinux/extlinux.conf; \
echo "=== NOTHING BOOTED - previous boot's ramoops log follows (ASCII column) ==="; \
md.b 0xe200000c 0x400
